id: ops-release-1020-recovery
title: Recover the historical 2026.10.20 GitHub Release with verified release evidence
description: "The read-only completeness audit reports exactly one missing GitHub Release: v2026.10.20. Its immutable release commit lacks the matching changelog section, so the automatic repair refuses it. The current changelog fix restores main and future release windows, but cannot alter that historical commit. Orchestrator must decide an evidence-preserving recovery path and separately authorize release creation; no tag, npm artifact or GitHub Release was changed here."
type: Task
pm_format_version: 2
status: in_progress
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-10-25T10:54:35.891Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-27T18:29:49.903Z"
assignee: glm-agent
claim_principal: glm-agent
author: codex-agent
comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-26T03:11:34.131Z",codex-agent,"Closure audit on main 1ceb53b69d4bb0eea323625744fe9200a38bb267: merged PR #205 (36478d717b40a5f73a49b06b486c1c83f93d39bc, merged 2026-10-25T11:22:43Z). REST GET releases/tags/v2026.10.20 returns HTTP 404. Inspected release-repair.ts and .github/workflows/release.yml; the changelog fix does not create a historical GitHub Release. Owner decision and separately authorized historical release recovery remain required. Leave open; no release or tag is changed. Existing behavioural revert evidence is retained; this tracker-only audit changes no runtime functions."
  "2026-10-27T02:55:57.395Z",codex-agent,"Reconfirmed for the release-changelog recurrence: git show v2026.10.20:CHANGELOG.md lacks the matching section, while v2026.10.26 and current main contain it exactly once. The repair error refers to immutable historical notes, not current main. This remains a separately authorized release-recovery task; ops-release-changelog-stability fixes pending-to-tag generation drift."
  "2026-10-27T03:02:46.663Z",codex-agent,"The exact release workflow audit command was re-run behind a GitHub GET-only guard: npm run verify:release-completeness -- --repair. It reported github_release_absent: v2026.10.20 and FAIL - release repair refused: committed changelog must have exactly one section for 2026.10.20, with exactly one failure. All source/provenance/tarball checks before historical-note extraction succeeded. No release or tag was created. A passing live audit still requires separately authorized historical recovery; changing current generator output cannot alter the immutable tagged file."
  "2026-10-27T15:46:32.467Z",glm-agent,"Root cause diagnosis (worktree at origin/main 089e803): the v2026.10.20 tag, npm 2026.10.20 dist.gitHead and the remote tag all name release commit ee029738 ('Release pm-ops v2026.10.20', authored by github-actions[bot]). That release commit bumped package.json, package-lock.json, manifest.json, index.ts and dist/index.js but did NOT touch CHANGELOG.md: git show v2026.10.20:CHANGELOG.md ends at the 2026.10.15 section - zero sections for 2026.10.20 (and none for 10.16-10.19). git log v2026.10.15..v2026.10.20 -- CHANGELOG.md is empty, so the 2026-10-20 run created its release commit during the changelog generation-drift window (the pending-to-tag drift fixed separately in ops-release-changelog-stability / PR #205, which restored main's changelog afterwards). Current main is healthy: the committed changelog has exactly one section for every one of the 61 release-tag versions, and npm view pm-ops@2026.10.20 dist.gitHead returns the same ee029738. The blocker is therefore NOT current main's changelog but the repair's single note source: repairReleaseCompleteness extracts notes only via git show <release-commit>:CHANGELOG.md, and that immutable commit can never gain a section, so every daily run's --repair refuses forever. Fix plan: keep the release commit's own changelog as the primary note source, and fall back to the audit-time committed changelog (regenerated by changelog:full --all-release-tags and enforced by changelog:check plus a new pre-release section gate) only when the historical commit lacks exactly one section; never read uncommitted working-tree state."
  "2026-10-27T18:16:52.191Z",glm-agent,"Revert proof (behavioural revert run in a disposable copy of the package worktree, never the checkout): with releaseNotesFor reverted to the pre-fix single-source read of the release commit's changelog, the two new repair tests fail ('a drift-window release commit without its own section recovers notes from the audit-time committed changelog' and 'a release missing its section in both committed changelogs still refuses before writes'); with the section gate's audit gutted and unwired from release:check, the gate tests fail ('the drift shape fails before a release starts', 'verify audits a checkout's changelog against injected tags' and 'the release gate chain runs the section check before a release starts'). All focused tests pass on the fixed implementation (release-repair.test.ts: 27/27; verify-release-changelog-sections.test.ts: 7/7)."
  "2026-10-27T18:23:16.360Z",glm-agent,"Local dry-run of the repair (the script has no dry-run flag, so the GitHub Release write boundary was stubbed; everything before the write ran for real from a scratch script, with npm reads on a clean config): the report-only audit shows exactly one failure ('release tag v2026.10.20 has no corresponding GitHub Release'); the repair run verified npm evidence for pm-ops@2026.10.20 (attested provenance, registry identity and gitHead match the tag commit), verified the published tarball against the release tag tree, verified the committed package identity, and then planned the release notes for v2026.10.20 as 'No tracked changes.' - the exact section from the audit-time committed changelog. The only remaining step was the GitHub Release write, which the daily release job's own --repair will perform on its next run; no tag, npm artifact or GitHub Release was changed here."
